        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Mesopotamian religion</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 3500 BCE – c. 500 CE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Origin</span><span class="info-stat-val">ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a> in the south; its undercurrents are believed to go back to the first half of the 6th millennium BCE, and its first clear evidence comes with the invention of writing around 3500 BCE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Founder or early teachers</span><span class="info-stat-val">none: a tradition governed by usage rather than by any official decision, which grew over millennia among Sumerian and Akkadian speakers</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Scriptures</span><span class="info-stat-val">no single scripture: myths, prayers, incantations and lists of gods written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> on clay tablets, in Sumerian, Akkadian or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a>, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enuma-elish">Enuma Elish</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epic-of-gilgamesh">Epic of Gilgamesh</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Followers today</span><span class="info-stat-val">none: it had almost completely died out by about 400–500 CE, after its indigenous adherents had largely become <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac-christians">Syriac Christians</a></span></div>
        </div>
