    <section class="kid-tale" aria-labelledby="kid-tale-title">
      <h2 class="kid-tale-title" id="kid-tale-title">The story of Mesopotamian religion</h2>
      <ol class="kid-tale-pages"><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cuneiform.jpg" alt="Cuneiform" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Cuneiform <span class="kid-tale-cr">William Albert Mason, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>More than 5,000 years ago, people in Mesopotamia invented writing, and with it comes the first clear sign of their religion.</p><p>They wrote prayers and stories of their gods on clay tablets, in a script called cuneiform.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/inanna.jpg" alt="Inanna" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Inanna <span class="kid-tale-cr">Sailko, CC BY 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>They believed in many gods, among them the goddess Inanna, the moon god Nanna and the sun god Utu.</p><p>They believed that people had been made to serve the gods.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/babylon.jpg" alt="Babylon" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Babylon <span class="kid-tale-cr">David Stanley, CC BY-SA 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>Every city had its own god, like Marduk at Babylon and Ashur at the city of Assur.</p><p>Each god was thought to live in its temple, where its statue was served meals twice a day.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/assur.jpg" alt="Assur" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Assur <span class="kid-tale-cr">Véronique Dauge, CC BY-SA 3.0 igo</span></figcaption></figure><p>Priests tried to learn the wishes of the gods from birds, dreams and the sky.</p><p>Ordinary people prayed to a personal god, who they believed would protect them.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hammurabi.jpg" alt="Hammurabi" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Hammurabi <span class="kid-tale-cr">Mbzt, CC BY 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>King Hammurabi of Babylon raised the god Marduk to be one of the greatest gods.</p><p>More than 1,500 years ago the old religion faded, as most of its people became Christians.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/epic-of-gilgamesh.jpg" alt="Epic of Gilgamesh" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Epic of Gilgamesh <span class="kid-tale-cr">CC0</span></figcaption></figure><p>Its Epic of Gilgamesh tells of a king, his wild friend Enkidu and his search to live forever.</p><p>Many stories in the Bible, such as the Flood, may have been based on these older myths.</p></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Every city had its patron god, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enki">Enki</a> at Eridu, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enlil">Enlil</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nippur">Nippur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marduk">Marduk</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> and Ashur at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assur">Assur</a>, and each god lived in a temple run as a household, its image fed, dressed and served.</li><li>Priests read the will of the gods in entrails, birds, the sky and dreams, and ordinary people prayed to a personal god and warded off demons with incantations.</li><li>It left the oldest body of recorded religious literature, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epic-of-gilgamesh">Epic of Gilgamesh</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enuma-elish">Enuma Elish</a>, and many biblical stories were possibly based on its myths.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Cult statues were brought to life in night rituals in which their mouths were “opened” and washed so that they could see and eat; while the god “ate” its twice-daily banquet, a curtain was drawn before the table, just as the king could not be seen eating.</p></aside>
    </section>

