    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 3500 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Writing is invented in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, bringing the first evidence of a recognisably Mesopotamian religion</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 2600 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">A Sumerian list of about 560 deities, found at Fara and Tell <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-salabikh">Abu Salabikh</a>, ranks five gods above the rest</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 2335 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon of Akkad</a> unites <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, and Sumerian and Akkadian gods are increasingly identified with one another</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">18th century BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> raises <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marduk">Marduk</a> to supremacy alongside Anu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enlil">Enlil</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 12th century BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enuma-elish">Enuma Elish</a> tells how <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marduk">Marduk</a> made the heavens and the earth from the body of Tiamat</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">539 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> conquers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> but leaves its native religion undisturbed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2nd century BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a> a revival in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> dedicates temples once more to Ashur, Sin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utu">Shamash</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadad">Hadad</a>, Bel and Ishtar</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 400–500</span><span class="rf-text">The old religion has almost completely died out, its people having largely become <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac-christians">Syriac Christians</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>

